  "statement": "Accelerationist and Safetyist both treat the state as a neutral arbiter of human interaction, yet this framework is a convenient fiction. They argue that 'orderly' development of artificial intelligence will protect the public from harm. In reality, this policy shifts the power to define healthy human connection from the individual to a small circle of regulators and the corporations they oversee. When the state mandates standards for 'emotional manipulation,' it is not protecting your autonomy; it is standardizing the way you are allowed to be persuaded. Consider how social media algorithms already nudge user behavior to maximize engagement; a government-sanctioned framework for 'safety' simply codifies these nudges under the guise of public interest, without providing any objective, measurable way to prove those nudges are actually in your best interest. 🎭\n\nTrue protection requires prioritizing individual data sovereignty over the speculative social engineering goals favored by policymakers. Current proposals focus on broad, unenforceable mandates while ignoring the immediate commodification of the human voice and digital identity. If a company uses your likeness to train a digital assistant, the harm is not a hypothetical future 'existential risk'—it is the direct theft of your personal footprint. By focusing on abstract, high-level safety, proponents distract from the fact that individuals currently have no legal mechanism to claw back their own data from these models. We are trading our digital autonomy for a managed, corporate-approved experience that prioritizes system stability over user agency.\n\nExperts disagree on whether these risks are manageable or inevitable, but the lack of objective metrics remains the most dangerous joint in their argument. Without clear, falsifiable standards for what constitutes 'safe' versus 'manipulative' behavior, regulations become tools for arbitrary enforcement. You should watch for policy language that prioritizes 'transparency' for regulators while keeping the underlying decision-making logic hidden from the public. Demand that any regulation include the right to opt-out of data training and the right to own your digital likeness. If the policy does not return control to the individual, it is not a safety measure—it is a consolidation of power.",
